      "id": "kpk1-l03-c1-t01",
      "before": "kpk1-l03-c1-e01",
      "title": {
        "en": "This, in three genders: ten, ta, to"
      },
      "body": {
        "en": "*Ten*, *ta* and *to* all mean \"this\", in the noun's gender. Every Polish noun is masculine, feminine or neuter (*rodzaj męski, żeński, nijaki*), and its ending usually tells you which. Most nouns ending in a consonant are masculine and take *ten*: *ten zeszyt* (a few, like *ta noc*, the night, are feminine). One in *-a* is feminine and takes *ta*: *ta torba*, but a few words for men end in *-a* too and take *ten*: *ten kolega*. One in *-o*, *-e*, *-ę* or *-um* is neuter and takes *to*: *to okno*, *to muzeum*. In *to jest…*, \"this is…\", *to* stays the same for every noun: *to jest torba*."
      },
      "table": {
        "title": {
          "en": "Ten, ta, to"
        },
